00:00:03.199 --> 00:00:04.230 align:start position:0%
 
for decades  people have been imagining what it means  for a machine to develop intelligence  a machine with many of the  characteristics of the human mind  this is not tomorrow's dream it is  equipment ready and available for use  today  if humans can use information to solve  problems  then maybe our technology can too  the term artificial intelligence  originally popped up in the 1950s  today ai is everywhere you look  it's targeting ads it's influencing  elections  it isn't just in the news it's how you  get your news  but as different ai technologies become  more and more  integrated into our daily lives should  we be worried about robots taking over  the planet  what are the key challenges facing  society today  and how do we find creative solutions to  tackle those challenges  [Music]  we sat down with some of canada's  leading researchers and policy experts  to ask them what we need to know about  ai these people are looking at the  impacts of ai on society  exploring everything from applications  in the operating room to autonomous cars  to bots writing news articles so they're  pretty used to the hype  i sometimes talk with a taxi driver and  i say oh i work in the eye and they're  like oh and terminator is the first  thing they think  which is a bit unfortunate because we're  definitely extremely far from anything  related to terminator  i think a lot of people think that ai is  this big thing that they cannot  understand  because of that they they are stuck in  the non-understanding part  in the old days when i brought up  artificial intelligence or that i worked  in artificial intelligence to other  people they usually ask  to see the robots the expected robots to  be there but ai has been  catching on a lot recently so they have  people have a better understanding of  the kinds of things that we do  people are asking about how do you how  do you handle bias in the data  how do you make sure that the technology  is safe before you put it into practice  so we're having much more kind of  advanced conversations around artificial  intelligence than ever before  all right so let's jump in  there are a ton of terms floating around  in the realm of ai  ai itself describes both the field of  research  and the capabilities of computer systems  maybe you have a coffee machine instead  of having to  do all of the individual steps to make a  coffee  the machine itself might boil the water  and force it through the beans  and then pour that into your cup those  are all individual things that you could  have done  but instead an algorithm and some  processes work together  and now you have a cup of coffee you can  extend that to  many other human processes  when you ask the average person how  often do you use ai technology and  the majority of them will say rarely or  never because they think of robots  but how many of us have smartphones you  use ai  when you use pretty much any application  there's sort of two big  categories of the way we think about ai  in the broadest sense  one is called narrow ai  that is artificial intelligence that is  developed to do  one specific task the other big category  is what's called artificial general  intelligence  and the idea behind this is that it's  something that  can kind of learn anything it's like a  person  and that is by most people's guesses a  very very long way away  right now we can train computer systems  to complete a task  and just like humans they do this by  learning from experience and improving  themselves along the way  when people refer to ai these days  they're usually talking about this  learning process  called machine learning machine learning  is how to program a computer  to learn from experience instead of  programming the computer to solve a task  directly you will program a computer to  give examples  the algorithm will learn how to solve  the task ah  yes the algorithm you probably hear that  word a lot these days  an algorithm is a set of instructions  that computer software follows to  complete a task  think of it like a recipe for a cake it  tells you step by step what you need to  do  with older systems people had to come up  with the recipe themselves  writing out each line of code to tell  the machine which ingredients and  quantities  to use but machine learning is different  instead of giving the system the recipe  we give it a lot of  examples of cake and program it to come  up with the recipe by itself  it continuously tests out different  combinations improving itself again and  again  as it tries to create the recipe that's  as close as possible to the one we want  you may have heard of another term that  goes one step further  a subfield of machine learning called  deep learning  in deep learning the system learns from  all of the previous recipes it knows  in order to predict a totally new recipe  it uses complex multi-layered structures  called neural networks this is  effectively very abstractly simulated  brains  so large networks of interconnected  units not  unlike the large networks of  interconnected cells that exist in our  brains  deep learning is how systems can  recognize multiple patterns in very  large data sets  it's used to develop things like natural  language processing  which is how computers understand human  language  this allows us to do things like  translate an entire page of text  in one instant it's also used for image  recognition  which is how your phone can recognize  that's you in that selfie  but none of this is possible without one  key element that all machine learning  relies on  data data is very important  the reason that we've seen the advances  that we have  across many application areas of machine  learning is because we  now have enough data and enough compute  we're now  finally at a place where we have enough  examples that we can  train the models robustly when training  computer systems  you can switch up the type and amount of  data you give your model  depending on how you want it to learn  there are several different techniques  or styles of learning  a popular one is reinforcement learning  reinforcement learning it's a subfield  of machine learning where  your algorithm it learns to do a task by  interacting with an environment  data allows machines to identify what  objects are  but even more importantly what they are  not  when humans are learning what it means  to be a dog  they can see millions of examples  of an individual dog walking around  different angles different lighting  a model only has what you give it unless  you deploy it in the wild  and so when we're training a visual  model for example  we have to give it as many examples of a  dog as we can  and importantly we have to give it a lot  of examples of not a dog  this cat is not a dog this cat from this  angle is not a dog this table is not a  dog this chair is not a dog  as we continue to provide more data our  systems will only become more  intelligent for those of us who aren't  spending our days training machines  we might not need to know the math  behind the models but as algorithms  become deeply integrated into our lives  we'll need to start considering the  impacts on both individuals  and society it will have direct impact  in your life very soon or it's already  having an impact  so i think it's good as a critical  minded citizen to be aware  of how things work the same way it's  good to be aware of climate change  ai isn't scary and it doesn't have to be  technical  you don't have to understand the  algorithms or the mathematics behind ai  you just need to understand what it's  used for  you

